# Production-build overrides. Loaded ONLY when NODE_ENV=production (i.e. `next build`), never by
# `next dev` — so .env.local keeps its localhost value for day-to-day development.
#
# Why this file has to exist, and why it has this exact name:
# Next resolves env vars in priority order, stopping at the first hit —
#   process.env  >  .env.production.local  >  .env.local  >  .env.production  >  .env
# Note that .env.local BEATS .env.production. So putting the real URL in .env.production would not
# work: a production build on a machine that has .env.local would still inline localhost. Only the
# .production.local name outranks it.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ values are inlined into the bundle at BUILD time, so a wrong value here cannot be
# corrected by editing an env var afterwards — it needs a fresh build.
#
# Contains no secrets by design. NEXT_PUBLIC_ variables are shipped to the browser and are public
# by definition, so this file is safe to include in a deploy zip. Every secret stays in .env.local
# (local) or the Hostinger panel (production).
#
# The Hostinger panel still overrides this, since panel variables arrive as process.env — priority 1.
# Set it there too; this file is the safety net for builds that happen anywhere else.

# No www. www.everlywhite.com 301-redirects to this origin.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://everlywhite.com
